  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nBetter forcing for ascent will overspread the area tonight through \nSaturday as the ridge to the east breaks down and subtle short waves \nare able to traverse the area. CAMs are agreement that falling \nheights associated with one wave late tonight will allow for enough \ninstability for convection while the placement and coverage is \nvariable amongst models. Some model solutions show little in the way \nof convection overnight while others show isolated convection could \nbring localized rainfall in excess of 3 inches in 3 hours which \nwould exceed flash flood guidance. Therefore it is encouraged that \npeople have multiple ways to receive a warning overnight tonight \nbecause isolated instances of flash flooding are possible.\n\nThe first wave exits east after day break with another wave poised \nto cross Saturday afternoon. Despite potential cloud cover, CAMs are \nin good agreement that there will be enough instability for \nconvection as this second wave crosses. Shear will be lacking \nreducing the threat for strong to severe storms while high PWATs \nwill support torrential rainfall and bring the threat for localized \nflash flooding. CAMs remain variable in the placement of convection \nwhile most generally agree that localized amounts of 3 inches in 3 \nhours may occur somewhere across the forecast area through Saturday \nevening. A multi model consensus places the highest PoPs across the \nmountains and foothills. Will have to monitor areas that receive \nrainfall through tonight as these areas will be more susceptible \nto the heavy rainfall threat through Saturday evening. \nConvection will wane Saturday evening as this second wave passes\neast with little in the way of rainfall expected Saturday \nnight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nThe heat and humidity bounce back on Sunday with a return to \nmore sunshine to close out the weekend. The muggy airmass keeps \nthe chance for showers and storms a possibility as a weak front\ndissipates while crossing New England. Temperatures return to \nthe upper 80s to low 90s south of the mountains again on Sunday \nand Monday.\n\nDew points climb into the low to mid 70s on Sunday, pushing \nheat indices into the low to mid 90s. But the dissipating front \ndoes look likely to begin the trend toward some drier air by \nMonday. Dew points look more likely to remain in the 60s on \nMonday, lessening the moistures' impact on the apparent \ntemperatures.\n\nThe gradual trend toward drier and relatively cooler conditions\nlooks to continue through the remainder of the week. Rather \nthan one big front bringing a change of airmasses, a series of \nweak fronts from early to mid week looks to gradually bring \ntemps back close to average by the end of the week. The dew \npoints also look to drop with each passing front, falling back \ninto the 50s by late in the week.\n\nWidespread 80s still look likely for much of mid to late week. \nBut with a drier airmass, heat indices don't look to be a \nconcern, and nighttime lows will trend more significantly cooler\nwith a return to 50s at night by late week.",
